# Target C-II — endpoint incidence sharpens inverse-`D` near-unit bandwidth to four rows

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the high-`t` exact-swap branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** selected O--O path coordinates in the one-sign strong-TB1 branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** crossed-row rhombus-center capsule lemma; pairwise-disjoint selected O--O centerline crosscuts; unit separation; five-row capsule theorem; Horn reduction; sharp all-residue Horn budget  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_four_row_endpoint_incidence_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The five-row capsule theorem is sharp for arbitrary `sqrt(3)/2`-separated points in the radius-`1/2` capsule of a unit segment. Crossed-row centers have more structure: each belongs to an actual unit-rhombus contact face while the endpoints of the short segment are source vertices. That endpoint incidence removes the abstract five-center configuration.

> **Four-row endpoint-incidence theorem.** If `x,y` are distinct source vertices and
> \[
> 1<\ell:=|x-y|\le1+\varepsilon,
> \qquad 0\le\varepsilon\le {1\over20},
> \]
> then
> \[
> \boxed{|\operatorname{reg}(x)-\operatorname{reg}(y)|\le4.}
> \tag{EI.1}
> \]
> Thus every inverse-`D` near-unit LOWER exact-swap blocker crosses at most four selected O--O rows for all sufficiently large `D`.

---

## 1. A nonincident source point is far from a rhombus center

Let `F` be a unit rhombus with center `c`. Write its vertices as

\[
c+p,\quad c-p,\quad c+q,\quad c-q,
\]

where

\[
p\perp q,
\qquad |p|^2+|q|^2=1.
\tag{EI.2}
\]

If a source point `z` is not a vertex of `F`, unit separation gives `|z-v|>=1` for every vertex. Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
4
&\le\sum_{v\in V(F)}|z-v|^2\\
&=|z-c-p|^2+|z-c+p|^2+|z-c-q|^2+|z-c+q|^2\\
&=4|z-c|^2+2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{|z-c|\ge {1\over\sqrt2}.}
\tag{EI.3}
\]

---

## 2. Nonincident crossed-row centers collapse to two pole clusters

Rigidly place

\[
x=(0,0),\qquad y=(\ell,0),\qquad \ell=1+\varepsilon.
\]

For every selected row separating `x` and `y`, choose a strict unit rhombus whose midpoint centerline meets `xy`, as in the crossed-row center-capsule argument. If its center is `c=(t,h)`, the length-one centerline gives

\[
\operatorname{dist}(c,xy)\le {1\over2}.
\tag{EI.4}
\]

Suppose the chosen rhombus is incident to neither endpoint. Equation (EI.3) applied to both endpoints gives

\[
t^2+h^2\ge {1\over2},
\qquad
(\ell-t)^2+h^2\ge {1\over2}.
\tag{EI.5}
\]

The nearest point of `xy` to `c` cannot be an endpoint, since that would make the corresponding endpoint-center distance at most `1/2`. Hence

\[
0\le t\le\ell,
\qquad |h|\le {1\over2}.
\]

Combining this with (EI.5),

\[
\boxed{{1\over2}\le t\le {1\over2}+\varepsilon}
\tag{EI.6}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{h^2\ge {1\over4}-\varepsilon-\varepsilon^2.}
\tag{EI.7}
\]

Put

\[
a=\sqrt{{1\over4}-\varepsilon-\varepsilon^2}.
\]

Since

\[
\left({1\over2}-a\right)\left({1\over2}+a\right)=\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2
\]

and `1/2+a>=1/2`,

\[
{1\over2}-a\le2(\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2).
\tag{EI.8}
\]

Two nonincident centers with the same sign of `h` therefore differ horizontally by at most `epsilon` and vertically by at most `2(epsilon+epsilon^2)`. At `epsilon<=1/20`, their squared distance is at most

\[
\varepsilon^2+4(\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2)^2
\le {541\over40000}
<{3\over4}.
\tag{EI.9}
\]

Distinct selected rows use strict rhombus faces with disjoint interiors. Each strict unit rhombus contains the open disk of radius `sqrt(3)/4` about its center, so distinct crossed-row centers satisfy

\[
|c-c'|\ge {\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{EI.10}
\]

Thus at most one nonincident center lies above `xy` and at most one below it:

\[
\boxed{N_{nonincident}\le2.}
\tag{EI.11}
\]

---

## 3. Endpoint-incident crossed rows contribute at most one per endpoint

Fix endpoint `x` and the ray from `x` through `y`. The relative interior of a rhombus midpoint centerline lies inside the rhombus; its endpoints are edge midpoints.

If the ray meets the relative interior of the centerline of a rhombus incident to `x`, the ray direction lies in the interior angular sector of that contact face at `x`. Distinct incident contact faces have disjoint interior sectors, so at most one such face occurs.

If the ray lies on a contact edge shared by two incident faces, a midpoint centerline can meet that boundary ray only at the midpoint of the shared contact edge. The audited selected O--O centerline crosscuts are pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts, so two selected rows cannot both use that midpoint.

Therefore

\[
N_x\le1,
\qquad N_y\le1.
\tag{EI.12}
\]

Together with (EI.11),

\[
N\le N_x+N_y+N_{nonincident}\le4,
\]

proving (EI.1).

---

## 4. Exact-swap and Horn consequences

A LOWER exact-swap blocker satisfies

\[
1<|x-y|\le1+q_i,
\qquad q_i=O(D^{-1}),
\]

so the four-row theorem applies eventually.

A single exact-swap Horn subsystem may therefore retain one residue class modulo five. With

\[
s_0={791\over320{,}000{,}000},
\]

the retained one-class density is

\[
\boxed{
\gamma_5={s_0\over10}
={791\over3{,}200{,}000{,}000}
=2.471875\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{EI.13}

Its basic Horn floors are

\[
N_{lower}>{\gamma_5\over4}D-o(D),
\qquad
N_{upper}>{\gamma_5\over8}D-o(D),
\qquad
N_{forest}>{\gamma_5\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{EI.14}

Using the current selected-path cap

\[
A_*={14\over3\sqrt3},
\]

the corresponding one-class UPPER flexible-localization coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
\widehat c_{*,5}
={\gamma_5\over48A_*}
={113\sqrt3\over102{,}400{,}000{,}000}
=1.91134512945\ldots\times10^{-9}.
}
\tag{EI.15}

Running the Horn reduction independently on all five residue classes and using the current repeated-UPPER rigidity gives the sharper source-wide weighted budget

\[
\boxed{
2N_L+N_U>
\left({s_0\over8}-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{EI.16}
\]

Consequently one metric type has density at least

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{sharp}={s_0\over24}
={791\over7{,}680{,}000{,}000}
=1.02994791666\ldots\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{EI.17}

The all-residue numerical improvement comes from the separate repeated-UPPER theorem; the contribution of the present theorem is that all LOWER objects in this sharp fork now have support depth at most four.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The short LOWER seam has been reduced as follows:

```text
whole crossed rhombi in a tube
    -> bandwidth <= 14;

rhombus centers + Oler / exact capsule packing
    -> bandwidth <= 6, then <= 5;

rhombus centers + source-endpoint incidence
    -> bandwidth <= 4.
```

The abstract five-point capsule packing remains valid, but five arbitrary capsule points cannot all be realized as crossed selected-row rhombus centers for a near-unit source pair. Nonincident centers are confined to two tiny pole clusters, and endpoint-incident rows are individually unique.

The remaining GLOBAL LOWER problem is a strict `1+O(D^-1)` source pair crossing at most four co-phased near-equilateral O--O rows. The global four-row ladder theorem uses injective ownership to repeat this gadget linearly many times against one fixed transverse train.

**Disposition:** proved the four-row endpoint-incidence bound, reduced valid exact-swap spacing from six to five, and sharpened the GLOBAL LOWER local seam to support depth at most four. C-II and Level I remain open.
